## A new era, a new layout

You’ve reached 2014. Most sites now open on phones, screen widths vary wildly, and float as a layout tool has worn everyone out. This is when browsers gain **flexbox** (the Flexible Box Layout). The first drafts of the specification date from 2009, and the final form you’ll use now settled around 2012 to 2014. It became common once every major browser supported it. For our practice that means no tables and no floating blocks: rows and columns line themselves up.

## The container and its items

Flexbox works like this: you declare a parent to be a **flex container**, and its children become **flex items**, which line up in a row or a column.

```css
.menu {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

Two lines, and the menu items stand in a row with space between them. Now two ideas that everything else rests on:

- the **main axis** is the direction the items run in. By default it goes left to right (`flex-direction: row`). Say `column`, and it goes top to bottom;
- the **cross axis** is perpendicular to the main one.

And two properties for the two axes: `justify-content` spreads items along the **main** axis, and `align-items` aligns them along the **cross** axis. When the direction changes, the axes swap places, and the properties swap jobs with them. That is the one thing people mix up here. Play with the diagram: change the direction and watch.

<FlexPlayground />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what changes if flex-direction goes from row to column?</summary>

The main axis becomes vertical, so justify-content spreads items from top to bottom, and align-items aligns them horizontally.

</details>

## Container properties

- `display: flex` turns flexbox on;
- `flex-direction: row | column` (and `-reverse`) says which way the items run;
- `flex-wrap: wrap` lets items move to the next line. Without it everything stays on one line and, if there isn’t enough room, shrinks or spills past the edge;
- `justify-content: flex-start | center | flex-end | space-between | space-around` spreads items along the main axis;
- `align-items: stretch | flex-start | center | flex-end` aligns them on the cross axis. The default, `stretch`, makes items as tall as their row, which is why cards in one row have equal heights;
- `gap: 1rem` is the space between items. It used to be done with margins, and you had to fight the extra margin after the last item. `gap` has worked for flexbox in all modern browsers since 2021.

## Item properties

The main property of an item is `flex`, a shorthand for three values:

```css
.card {
  flex: 1 1 14rem;
}
```

These are **flex-grow** (can it grow and take free space?), **flex-shrink** (can it shrink?) and **flex-basis** (the size it would like before growing or shrinking). `flex: 1 1 14rem` reads: “I want a width of 14rem, but I can grow to fill the row and I can shrink.” That is how you get cards that wrap onto the next line by themselves: four in a row on a wide screen, one on a phone, without a single line about the screen.

There is also `align-self` (align one item differently) and `order` (change the order). Be careful with the second: it changes only how things look, while the keyboard and screen reader order stays the same. Eyes and narrator part ways. It’s better to change the order of the items in the HTML itself.

Three ready-made tricks are worth remembering:

- a header with the name on the left and the menu on the right: `justify-content: space-between`;
- centering a block on both axes (a task once thought a joke): `justify-content: center; align-items: center`;
- pushing one item to the right: `margin-left: auto`.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what does flex: 1 1 14rem do?</summary>

The item wants a width of 14rem, but it can grow to fill the free space and shrink if there isn’t enough.

</details>

## Practice

A new era, a new page: in 2014 your site is built from scratch, but with no styling yet. Your `styles.css` already holds the base, what people in 2014 called a “reset”: sizes, fonts, colors. Your job is the layout.

Because every era is built from scratch, the stylesheet has to be linked again. It’s the same line as in 2005. After that you lay out the header, the menus, the main and side blocks and the cards with flexbox. Try the result yourself: drag the edge of the browser window and check that the menus and cards wrap instead of spilling over the edge.

## What was wrong with 2014

The new layout was good, but it was still written in “desktop” sizes: widths in pixels, images with no fallback size. In the next lessons you’ll make it responsive. There was another catch: the first flexbox had two different syntaxes, and Internet Explorer 10 understood a third, so authors wrote every variant one after another. That habit left with those browsers.
